  ('daa8b40a-98d4-4533-bcfb-87fbd1359024', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'bd32357e-c161-4052-abdf-a267b5139644', 'aedc9bf9-5810-404d-af31-095336b601fc', 'd9ec752e-e972-447c-b099-ccf502d554ab', '41e0a701-bdda-403b-bf4d-003aaffa605b', $qbx$Haryana’s per capita income is significantly higher than both Kerala and Bihar. Despite this, the IMRs of Haryana (28) and Bihar (27) remain high compared to Kerala's lower rate (6). This suggests that while economic development can provide resources, factors such as healthcare access and quality may still be lacking.$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-05-22T05-56-28-173Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('4afb71ba-6e2d-4aee-8b50-aeebc73bdb67', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'bd32357e-c161-4052-abdf-a267b5139644', 'aedc9bf9-5810-404d-af31-095336b601fc', 'd9ec752e-e972-447c-b099-ccf502d554ab', '41e0a701-bdda-403b-bf4d-003aaffa605b', $qbx$Haryana’s per capita income is significantly higher than that of Bihar at Rs 3,25,759 compared to Rs 60,337. Despite this difference, Kerala still has a relatively high IMR of 6, indicating that economic development alone does not fully address health issues.$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-05-22T05-56-28-173Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('583bdb03-2d14-46af-8bf1-9e9fbcd29a17', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'bd32357e-c161-4052-abdf-a267b5139644', 'aedc9bf9-5810-404d-af31-095336b601fc', 'd9ec752e-e972-447c-b099-ccf502d554ab', '41e0a701-bdda-403b-bf4d-003aaffa605b', $qbx$Haryana’s per capita income is significantly higher than both Kerala and Bihar at Rs 3,25,759 compared to Rs 60,337. Despite this difference, Kerala still has a relatively high IMR of 6, indicating that economic development alone does not fully address health issues.$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-05-22T05-56-28-173Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('a4305810-d251-4a26-aec3-7501cd5d7586', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'bd32357e-c161-4052-abdf-a267b5139644', 'aedc9bf9-5810-404d-af31-095336b601fc', 'd9ec752e-e972-447c-b099-ccf502d554ab', '41e0a701-bdda-403b-bf4d-003aaffa605b', $qbx$The table reveals stark contrasts between Haryana and Kerala regarding infant mortality rates, per capita income, and school attendance ratios for students aged 15-17 years. In Haryana, nearly one-third of children in this age group did not attend school due to their parents' inability to provide education, highlighting the significant disparity in developmental metrics compared to the well-developed state of Kerala.$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-05-22T05-56-28-173Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('61023646-967c-47dd-a68d-d2c7d1a7114b', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'bd32357e-c161-4052-abdf-a267b5139644', 'aedc9bf9-5810-404d-af31-095336b601fc', 'd9ec752e-e972-447c-b099-ccf502d554ab', '41e0a701-bdda-403b-bf4d-003aaffa605b', $qbx$In Haryana, despite a higher per capita income compared to Kerala, there is a significant disparity in terms of child mortality rates and educational facilities. The literacy rate and number of children attending school in secondary classes are notably lower in Haryana than in Kerala. This highlights the inadequacy of income as an indicator for material goods and services that citizens can access.$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-05-22T05-56-28-173Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL);
